                <pb facs="0582" n="468" sameAs="frus:frus1931v01/pg468"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">500.A15 Arms Truce/55: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in Switzerland</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Wilson</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Geneva</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1931-09-29T16:00:00+01:00">September 29, 1931—4
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received September 29—1:50 p.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>172. My 171, September 29, 11 a.m. Assembly adopted resolution and report of
                    Third Committee this morning.</p>
                <p>[Paraphrase.] As of possible interest to you, Grandi considers that although a
                    very different kind of truce would have been preferable, a really valuable step
                    in establishing confidence has been taken. Moreover, he is impressed by the
                    conciliatory attitude of certain Governments which, at the start, were clearly
                    reluctant to take any step and he is satisfied that his negotiators accomplished
                    what was possible. [End paraphrase.]</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Wilson</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
